#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192337 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "00b0d838-163d-5299-beaf-255f9b13cf71" :question/math-academy-id "e-11034" :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined by\n$A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2+y^2 \\geq 4 \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A - \\partial A = \\textrm{int}\\, A$\n2. $\\textrm{int}\\, A \\cap \\partial A = \\emptyset$\n3. $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A$, denoted $\\partial A$, is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A$.\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4063/Source/Images/3933c33c218efa0a3c94b16611464247.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A$, denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, A$, is the set containing all the interior points of $A$. It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = A - \\partial A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4063/Source/Images/b920371607ced7ab80764cc5816233ad.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A - \\partial A = \\textrm{int}\\, A$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\textrm{int}\\, A \\cap \\partial A = \\emptyset$, where $\\emptyset$ denotes the empty set.\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a8045dd8-90b8-5671-9003-2539c502cb49" :question/math-academy-id "e-11035" :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined by\n$A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, y^2 - 1 \\leq x, \\, -2 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$.\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n$\\textrm{int}\\,A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}, \\, -2 < y < 2\\big\\}$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A$, denoted $\\partial A$, is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A$.\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4063/Source/Images/f093d44e5e20a955b22580fb3ab0dd29.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A$, denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, A$, is the set containing all the interior points of $A$. It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = A - \\partial A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4063/Source/Images/62b88a35fdee44917ca70f5ed7c8d35b.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{y^2 - 1 < x}, \\, -2 < y < 2\\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "44971e25-0367-5b13-a6be-db503c6b4539" :question/math-academy-id "e-12399" :question/problem "Consider the set $A$, defined by\n$A = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2: x^2+y^2 -1<0\\}$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n$\\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,: \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}} \\, \\big\\}$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A$, denoted $\\partial A$, is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A$.\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4063/Source/Images/e005d0c3f8da9cb02b75aca3b6b27a91.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the circle\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{x^2+y^2 = 1} \\, \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e534c006-a5b4-5f76-bbd6-451496358d3a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Boundary of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819ad07f-30a8-560a-823e-cc39b532b055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} > 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190916} {:answer/id #uuid "8edb4482-ff3c-5a51-9cfe-12eae815fd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} < 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190917} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4b5bf9-83e1-55b2-86b2-a9505998428b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} \\le 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190918} {:answer/id #uuid "000e1228-4f30-5e96-8cb2-f92e6a06377b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} \\ge 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190919} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6800af-f876-5f20-8ac9-78af6242f8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e6800af-f876-5f20-8ac9-78af6242f8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190920} :answer-field/id #uuid "21bf9d21-1513-55c7-8d72-9cd054a73e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190915}] :question/id #uu